WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.810
The Goldfinch enthralled readers after its
Pulitzer-winning release as an acclaimed novel,

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:08.870
and now it's poised to have the same effect
on filmgoers as it arrives in theaters in

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:09.640
2019.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.460
Wondering when you can see it, who's starring
in it, and who's behind the camera?

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.120
Here's everything you need to know.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.870
Fans of Donna Tartt's work have grown accustomed
to waiting — her shortest gap between novels

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.950
is the ten-year drought that spanned between
her 1992 debut The Secret History and its

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.480
follow-up, The Little Friend — so the fact
that The Goldfinch has taken six years to

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.220
make its way to the big screen is almost to
be expected.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.640
"I've tried to write faster and I don't really
enjoy it.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.340
I don't enjoy the process of doing it, I've
tried to speed up."

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.620
But for those who've been eager to see this
story brought to life in theaters, that patience

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:47.610
will soon be rewarded: The Goldfinch is scheduled
to arrive on September 13th, 2019.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.079
A fall release date for a major motion picture
tends to signal the studio's belief that the

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.500
film in question could be a contender during
awards season.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.559
After blockbuster season fades in August,
audiences tend to be in the mood for more

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:02.559
serious fare — and awards voters are also
more likely to remember a movie that earned

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.700
acclaim later in the year.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.600
Whether this confidence pays off after critics
have their say remains to be seen, but this

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.800
release date bodes well for the way studio
execs feel about The Goldfinch.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.840
When it came time to cast The Goldfinch's
main character, Theo, the minds behind the

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.670
film knew they would have to cast someone
who could shoulder every bit of the protagonist's

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:25.399
intense, harrowing emotional journey — and
thanks to a breakout role in a 2017 summer

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.320
hit by Edgar Wright, Ansel Elgort ended up
nabbing this huge role.

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:33.499
The actor had been making waves for some time,
appearing in films like The Fault in Our Stars

00:01:33.499 --> 00:01:38.079
and the Divergent film series, but according
to Variety, it was his quiet yet emotional

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.520
turn in Baby Driver that got him enough attention
to book the role of Theo.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.139
Elgort and The White Queen's Aneurin Barnard
will serve as the film's two main stars, and

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:49.119
the rest of the cast is appropriately A-list.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:53.579
Perhaps the biggest name is Oscar- and Emmy-winning
star Nicole Kidman, who has won critical acclaim

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.340
for everything from Moulin Rouge to Big Little
Lies.

00:01:56.529 --> 00:02:00.940
You'll also see Emmy winner Sarah Paulson,
known for her work on American Horror Story,

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.569
as well as recognizable faces like American
Horror Story's Denis O'Hare, Old School's

00:02:05.569 --> 00:02:08.860
Luke Wilson, and Stranger Things actor Finn
Wolfhard.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.040
With the film rights up for sale, Hollywood
started vying in earnest for the chance to

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.629
score Tartt's first-ever adaptation, and in
2014 — a year after the book's release and

00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:22.739
shortly after it won the Pulitzer Prize — a
major studio stepped in to purchase the rights

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.640
for The Goldfinch.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.620
According to Deadline, the book, which at
this point had spent dozens of weeks on The

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.849
New York Times' bestseller list, was snapped
up by Warner Brothers Studios in conjunction

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:33.269
with RatPac Entertainment.

00:02:33.269 --> 00:02:36.840
It made sense at the time for Warner Brothers
to pick up the rights, especially since they

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.159
had previously purchased the rights to The
Secret History, which was never made into

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:40.660
a movie.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.879
With The Secret History still awaiting development
and its award-winning successor primed for

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:47.870
the screen, The Goldfinch became the immediate
focus.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.780
When it came time to choose a director for
The Goldfinch, the studio settled on John

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.640
Crowley, an Irish director with a strong background
in theater.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:00.220
Crowley's breakout film came in 2003 with
Intermission, which won several filmmaking

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:05.170
awards in Ireland, but he hit even bigger
with 2015's Brooklyn, an Academy Award nominee

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.239
for Best Picture.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.760
An enormous awards season hit, Brooklyn even
won the BAFTA for Best British Film.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.080
Considering Crowley has plenty of experience
directing personal yet epic stories that span

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:20.019
years and continents, it seems only appropriate
that he would be the right choice for The

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:23.760
Goldfinch, a film which makes its way across
the world while still remaining introspective

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:24.280
and personal.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.120
In 2017, Warner Brothers announced that it
was teaming up with Amazon Studios to partner

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:33.840
on The Goldfinch, meaning Amazon would invest
a significant chunk of the film's budget — reportedly

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.680
totaling close to $40 million, according to
Variety — in exchange for streaming rights

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.360
on their Prime service after the film's theatrical
release.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.450
Though many studios aggressively lobbied to
be a part of this partnership, Amazon won

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.940
out, and that may be due to more than their
sizable financial contribution.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:53.120
Thanks to Manchester by the Sea, an Amazon
Studios-produced indie that scooped up multiple

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:57.549
Academy Awards, the streaming service became
the first to score an Oscar nomination for

00:03:57.549 --> 00:04:01.500
Best Picture, which could only potentially
bode well for The Goldfinch's future.

